  "theory": {
        "intro": "In a right-angled triangle the two acute angles add to $90^{\\circ}$, so each is the other's complement. Swapping them swaps the opposite and adjacent sides, so every ratio of $\\theta$ equals the co-ratio of $90^{\\circ}-\\theta$.\n\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\\large}]\n\\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes, calc}\n\\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (B) at (4.8,0);\n\\coordinate (C) at (4.8,2.9);\n\\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway, below] {$b$};\n\\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway, right] {$a$};\n\\draw (C) -- (A) node[midway, above left] {$c$};\n\\coordinate (p) at ($(B)!0.3cm!(A)$);\n\\coordinate (q) at ($(B)!0.3cm!(C)$);\n\\draw (p) -- ($(p)+(q)-(B)$) -- (q);\n\\pic[draw, \"$\\theta$\", angle radius=0.8cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = B--A--C};\n\\pic[draw, \"$90^{\\circ}-\\theta$\", angle radius=0.75cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = A--C--B};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]",
        "facts": [
          "Two angles are **complementary** when they add to $90^{\\circ}$, so $A+B=90^{\\circ}$.",
          "$\\sin(90^{\\circ}-\\theta)=\\cos\\theta$ and $\\cos(90^{\\circ}-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta$.",
          "$\\tan(90^{\\circ}-\\theta)=\\cot\\theta$ and $\\cot(90^{\\circ}-\\theta)=\\tan\\theta$, provided $\\theta\\neq 0^{\\circ}, 90^{\\circ}$.",
          "$\\sec(90^{\\circ}-\\theta)=\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta$ and $\\operatorname{cosec}(90^{\\circ}-\\theta)=\\sec\\theta$, provided $\\theta\\neq 0^{\\circ}, 90^{\\circ}$.",
          "If a ratio equals its co-ratio, such as $\\sec A=\\operatorname{cosec} B$ or $\\tan A=\\cot B$, then $A+B=90^{\\circ}$.",
          "Complementary co-ratios divide to $1$, as $\\dfrac{\\sec 20^{\\circ}}{\\operatorname{cosec} 70^{\\circ}}=1$, and subtract to $0$, as $\\cot 35^{\\circ}-\\tan 55^{\\circ}=0$."
        ]
      },
